# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/

# Environment
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
**/.env
**/.env.local
# Any other .env suffix (.env.production, .env.staging, .env.bak, .env.txt ...).
# The list above enumerated three suffixes and missed every other one.
.env.*
**/.env.*
# Files that merely END in .env (production.env, local.env). A file with this
# ending is an env file in every case we can think of, so excluding it is safe.
*.env
# Shell-mangled env copies. A miskeyed PowerShell `Rename-Item .env .env.txt`
# once produced a file literally named "Rename-Item .env.txt" holding a live
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; none of the rules above matched it. This catches that shape.
# It does NOT make .gitignore a defence against arbitrary mangled names — only
# never having a real key on disk in this tree does that.
*.env.txt
# .env.example files are the tracked, placeholder-only templates. Re-included
# last so the broadened rules above cannot start hiding them.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Fixor pilot JSON store
data/
!data/.gitkeep

# OS / editors
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Editor / tooling backup copies, at any depth. A .bak file is almost always a
# copy of something that was mid-edit, so it inherits whatever that file held —
# on a public repo that is the whole risk. No tracked file matches this rule and
# none ever has (verified across all refs), so nothing is hidden by adding it.
#
# Two limits, stated so nobody assumes more cover than this gives:
#   - it matches the .bak SUFFIX only. Timestamped variants (foo.bak.20260725)
#     end in the timestamp, not in .bak, and are NOT caught here; the root
#     CLAUDE.md.bak* rule below covers that shape for that one file only.
#   - if a secrets-exposure fixture ever needs a real .bak name (leftover-backup-
#     in-webroot is a genuine vuln class this product could detect), negate it
#     with !fixtures/**/*.bak rather than dropping this rule.
*.bak

# Local scan outputs
scan-report-*.md

# Generated artifacts (e.g. sample audit PDFs from scripts/build-*-pdf.cjs).
# The generator scripts are tracked; their binary output is not.
outputs/

# Claude Code local state
.claude/

# Audit patch artifacts
*.patch

# Out-of-tree research / lead-gen data
leads/

# Outreach kit — prospect PII + outreach playbook, kept local only.
# Never commit: public repo + third-party personal data (privacy / Apollo ToS),
# and a publicly findable cold-email template kills the cold-email campaign.
outreach/

# Local smoke-test artifacts (cloned repos, scan reports, debug logs)
test-output/

# Internal-only docs — never publish.
# Strategy/prospect/audit material that would either embarrass us if leaked
# (audit honesty defects, prospect call-outs) or undercut us strategically
# (drafts of disclosure or launch posts published before coordination).
# Public docs that describe real product state belong in docs/ proper —
# detector-capabilities.md is the model.
docs/*-DRAFT.md
docs/FIXOR-CURRENT-STATE-*.md
docs/STATUS-SYNC-*.md
docs/publish-day-materials.md
# OWASP-DISCLOSURE: reconstructed from the founder's private Gmail — third-party
# names/addresses + strategy framing. WHAT-FIXOR-IS: internal orientation/audit
# doc; cites gitignored docs and outreach/ material and records launch status.
docs/OWASP-DISCLOSURE.md
docs/WHAT-FIXOR-IS.md

# Temporary PR body files (created when piping rich markdown to gh pr create
# via --body-file; safe to delete after the PR opens).
.pr-body-tmp.md

# Corpus-measurement scratch dumps written to the REPO ROOT by the zero-spend
# structural measurement work (measure:idor-structure, measure:icp-reach,
# scripts/source-icp-corpus.mjs). Two shapes, both named
# <two-letter-repo-tag>_<what>: GitHub API payloads (commit, compare, history)
# and third-party source files pulled out of the corpus repos for inspection.
# Neither is ours to publish, and this is a public repo.
#
# Both patterns are ROOT-ANCHORED (leading /) and key on an underscore in the
# filename, which is what keeps them narrow. This repo names files kebab-case
# everywhere (lint-no-console.mjs, source-icp-corpus.mjs, drizzle.config.ts), so
# an underscore in a ROOT filename is itself the anomaly signal:
#   - no first-party TypeScript lives at the repo root (it lives in src/, apps/,
#     scripts/); the only tracked root .ts is drizzle.config.ts;
#   - the tracked root JSON is package.json, package-lock.json, railway.json,
#     tsconfig.json;
#   none contain an underscore, and nothing outside the root is touched — an
#   underscored .json or .ts in src/, docs/ or fixtures/ stays fully visible.
# Residual breadth, stated rather than hidden: a future ROOT file named with an
# underscore would be silently ignored. Move the exception into the tree, or
# negate it here, rather than widening these two lines.
/*_*.json
/*_*.ts

# The ROOT CLAUDE.md is deliberately kept out of git. This is the DECISION and its
# reasoning, recorded here so it is not re-litigated from scratch.
#
# This is a PUBLIC repository for a security product. That file collects the
# operational shape of the product into one place: which spend guards exist and
# how they are bypassed, the budget-cap defaults, the rate limiter's behaviour,
# which detectors do not call the model on their shipped path, and the distance
# between the README's claims and the tracker's NOT-READY verdict.
#
# The counter-argument was considered and is real: an ignored CLAUDE.md never
# reaches a fresh clone or a .claude/worktrees/ checkout, so the guards it carries
# (above all: never re-record replay fixtures to make a failing gate pass) do not
# load there.
#
# It loses on ASYMMETRY, not on size. A file missing from a fresh clone is
# repaired by copying it in. Operational detail published to a public
# security-product repo cannot be unpublished: forks, clones, caches and scrapers
# keep it after any deletion or history rewrite. Reversible cost beats
# irreversible cost. That is why this rule stands, even though the gap it creates
# is genuine.
#
# If the worktree gap starts to bite in practice, the remedy is to commit the
# public-facts half — safety rules, conventions and traps, all of which are
# already readable in tracked source — and keep only machine-state notes local.
# Not to unanchor this rule.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED (leading /) on purpose. apps/dashboard/CLAUDE.md is tracked and
# must stay tracked: it is a one-line "@AGENTS.md" pointer carrying Next.js
# version-drift notes and no safety content. An unanchored CLAUDE.md rule would
# silently stop that file from reaching a fresh clone.
/CLAUDE.md
# Timestamped local backups (CLAUDE.md.bak, CLAUDE.md.bak.20260725-1530, ...).
# Note there is NO general *.bak rule in this file, so this covers CLAUDE.md
# backups only; other .bak files anywhere in the tree remain visible to git.
/CLAUDE.md.bak*
